ed to train  differently from just training for your  max strength obviously one way you can  do this is to lower the weight  or perform something like lots and lots  of push-ups however if you really want  to train slow twitch fiber you also need  to go slowly or include isometric holds  and the reason for this is once again  that henneman size principle remember  you recruit the size of motor unit the  size of twitch muscle fiber that you  need in order to exert the amount of  force necessary to complete the task so  if you're doing a fast explosive push-up  you're going to need more fast twitch  muscle fiber when you run out of steam  when those fast twitch muscle fibers  fatigue you won't be able to perform any  more repetitions if you're going very  slowly however you don't need those fast  twitch muscle fibers and therefore you  can go past the point where you'd  otherwise fail and start to really  challenge the slow twitch fiber another  option is to use isometrics we are  simply holding a position just being a  yielding isometric this time so you  might just get into a a low halfway down  push-up position and hold that pose  until you completely collapse and once  again because you're just holding a  position you can use your slow twitch  fibers to go for as long as you possibly  can and a lot of calisthenics athletes  unknowingly train primarily slow twitch  fibers which is why you might see some  of them not be quite as explosive  despite having that incredible strength  control but my favorite strategy that i  like to use to train all my muscle  fibers is the drop set so let's say  you're performing as many repetitions as  you can of a bench press you could do  maybe you know five reps of 120  kilograms then you're going to drop down  immediately to say 100 kilograms to  perform a few more reps drop down  immediately to say 50 kilograms and  perform as many as you can 25 kilograms  and then just keep going to failure  using a slow tempo and lots of contro